WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Why did closing human genome gaps take from 2001 until 2022?

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.400
Two drafts appeared in February 2001.

3
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.700
Every chromosome except Y ran end to end without a gap in 2022.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.400
Next-generation sequencing transformed DNA reading, yet reading alone falls short.

5
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:31.997
Each Sanger reaction reads one region, commonly up to about 800 to 1,000 bases.

6
00:00:33.477 --> 00:00:37.617
Short-read sequencing reads hundreds of millions of fragments at once.

7
00:00:38.937 --> 00:00:43.657
Genomic DNA breaks into fragments a few hundred bases long, fitted with adapters,

8
00:00:44.337 --> 00:00:45.357
short known end pieces.

9
00:00:46.797 --> 00:00:51.517
Each fragment copies on a glass flow cell into a cluster of identical copies.

10
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.460
Now every cluster runs an add, image, reset cycle.

11
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.720
Each nucleotide carries a dye and a reversible terminator on its 3' end.

12
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.060
That removable block pauses copying after one base.

13
00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.380
Imaging logs each cluster's colour; cleaving removes dye and block.

14
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.280
Regenerating the 3' hydroxyl starts the next cycle.

15
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:23.177
Repeating cycles yields a read, the base sequence of one DNA fragment.

16
00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:28.437
Software aligns or assembles millions of reads along the chromosome.

17
00:01:30.017 --> 00:01:33.817
Genome results depend on what each read records and where it fits.

18
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.320
Now suppose a short read matches a repetitive sequence appearing in several places.

19
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.100
If a faster machine collects far more short reads, will the assembly gaps close?

20
00:01:51.717 --> 00:01:55.617
No, because reporting a base is not locating it in a genome.

21
00:01:57.257 --> 00:02:02.137
Short reads from repetitive sequence fit multiple spots, leaving gaps between unique

22
00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:08.197
stretches. Faster machines alone could not close those gaps until 2022.

23
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:13.837
Even when reads align cleanly, one sample can miss a rare variant.

24
00:02:15.057 --> 00:02:19.097
An apparently new variant in a child may hide in some parental cells.

25
00:02:20.397 --> 00:02:26.077
In 58 families, deep sequencing across several parental tissues caught this germline

26
00:02:26.077 --> 00:02:27.497
mosaicism in 5.

27
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.900
Read evidence alone does not say what a variant means for a person.

28
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.040
A change may be a driver giving a selective advantage, or a passenger giving none.

29
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.700
Comparing before-treatment and relapse sequences still requires testing whether the

30
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.480
mutant enzyme resists a drug.

31
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.670
With those safeguards, parallel sequencing unlocked discoveries across biology.

32
00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:00.930
In 2008, David Bentley and colleagues sequenced a human genome with this Illumina

33
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:07.810
method. In 2014, Knelman and colleagues sequenced bacteria in Puca Glacier soil

34
00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.610
exposed about three years.

35
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:16.330
Plots given nitrogen and phosphorus for one year most resembled soils exposed about

36
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.550
85 years. Plots given nitrogen and phosphorus for one year, most resembled soils

37
00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.350
exposed about 85 years.

38
00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:22.213
That shift was in one glacier's soils, not a whole ecosystem.

39
00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:29.993
In 2020, single-sperm sequencing of 20 human donors tested whether one crossover

40
00:03:29.993 --> 00:03:32.093
makes another nearby less likely.

41
00:03:33.293 --> 00:03:38.073
It detected crossover interference in every donor, confirming what Sturtevant

42
00:03:38.073 --> 00:03:44.133
suspected. Parallel reads unlock genomes only when we prove where each fragment

43
00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:44.573
belongs.
